<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Poljica</h1><p class="st-meta">1440 CE – 1807 CE (approx.) · capital Gata</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Self-governing peasant and minor-noble community between Split and Omiš that kept its own law and elected rulers for nearly four centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poljica">Poljica</a>, a hilly district between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetina">Cetina</a> river and the coast near Split, had long enjoyed local autonomy under Croatian and Hungarian kings. Its customary law was written down in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poljica-statute">Poljica Statute</a>, traditionally dated to 1440, which fixed the rights of its villages and noble families. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> advanced, Poljica placed itself under Venetian protection.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each year an assembly at Gata elected a grand knez from the local nobility, assisted by village knezes, judges and a chancellor. Land was held by kin groups, and the statute regulated inheritance, feuds and grazing. The statute was written in Croatian in Bosnian Cyrillic script. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poljica">Poljica</a> paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> while serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> as a frontier buffer.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> fell in 1797, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poljica">Poljica</a> came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and then, in 1806, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. When Poljica men joined a Russian-backed rising, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auguste-de-marmont">Marshal Marmont</a> abolished the republic in 1807 and merged it into French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a>. The statute remains a key source for Croatian law.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-poljica.jpg" alt="Republic of Poljica" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: geographicus.com · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1852_S.D.U.K._Pocket_Map_of_the_Balkans,_Croatia,_Dalmatia,_Sclavonia_-_Geographicus_-_AustrianDominionsIII-Knight-1852.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1440 CE – 1807 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gata</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Croatian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Poljica Principality, Poljička knežija</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1624&amp;focus=republic-of-poljica" aria-label="Republic of Poljica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5082 636 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Poljica lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5402 892L5293 831M5402 892L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5293 831h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5402 892h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5402 892h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Gata. <a href="/atlas?year=1624&amp;focus=republic-of-poljica">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

